# Every directory pattern here is anchored with a leading slash.
#
# Without the anchor, ignore-file semantics match a name at ANY depth: a bare
# `supabase` also matched `src/integrations/supabase/`, which silently dropped
# the Supabase client from the upload and failed the build on Vercel with
# "Could not load .../integrations/supabase/client". Anchoring keeps each rule
# to the directory it means.

# Secrets. The CLI uploads the working directory, so anything holding a
# credential is excluded. The build reads its VITE_* values from the project's
# Environment Variables; the 33 non-VITE keys in .env (Azure, Supabase service
# role, Railway, GitHub) never leave this machine.
/.env
/.env.local
/.env.development
/.env.production

# The analyst service is not deployed here. A run holds an SSE stream open for
# about 380 seconds and keeps its state in process; a Vercel function is capped
# at 300 seconds and has no sticky instance, so runs would be cut off and
# approvals would land on the wrong worker. It is hosted separately as a
# long-lived process (ai/Dockerfile, render.yaml).
/ai

# Database migrations and edge functions: deployed with Supabase, not Vercel.
/supabase

# Not needed to build or serve the frontend. Vercel runs the build itself, so a
# local dist/ would only inflate the upload and risk shipping a stale bundle.
/dist
/docs
/fixtures
/.lovable
/bun.lockb
/bun.lock
